Closes #3

## What landed

One tenant-aware resolution model for compiled and approved **imported** actions. A name resolves compiled-first, then through the caller tenant's approved `action_registry` rows inside a scoped transaction, and both materialise into one owned `ResolvedAction` (`crates/actions/src/resolve.rs`). The imported path runs the **same** external machinery as a compiled one — idempotency claim, circuit breaker, tenant credential, artefact materialisation and invocation bookkeeping — through an `ExternalCall` descriptor, so there is no second invocation path.

- `invoke()` resolves an uncompiled name itself; every HTTP and MCP call reaches the same function, so the transports cannot diverge.
- `resolve::precheck` authorises with the tenant's scope policy and validates the stored JSON schema; `_version`/`If-Match` folding is shared with the compiled path.
- Boot (`resolve::assert_boot_registry`, called from `main.rs`) accepts an approved imported row and refuses only an imported workflow or a service with no configured base URL.
- Discovery carries the tenant's imported actions in `actions.list`, `GET /api/actions`, `actions.describe`, and MCP `tools/list`; `tools/call` resolves them and refuses a non-injective MCP name as ambiguous.
- The external deadline now covers the response **body** (`send_once`), not just `send()`.
- Path/query/body bindings and the tenant credential are resolved from the row's `runtime`/`bindings` block; `CallContext` replaces `HandlerCtx` at the external boundary so no imported name has to be `'static` (document attribution follows the same split).

## The proof

`crates/actions/tests/action_resolution.rs` — one end-to-end test with the real stub service, over a real database and both transports:

1. import → approve through the production ingest path (`widgets.list`, not compiled);
2. the boot rule accepts it;
3. the HTTP manifest and the MCP tool list both carry it;
4. HTTP invocation: path `a b` → `/probe/a%20b`, query `x y`, and the upstream receives `Bearer s3cret` opened from the tenant's sealed secret;
5. MCP `tools/call` returns the same output as HTTP;
6. after a **restart** it still resolves and invokes;
7. a revoked approval answers `404 action_unavailable`, absent from the manifest and the tool list;
8. a breaking refresh leaves v2 unapproved and v1 still serving;
9. an imported workflow row is refused at boot;
10. a body stalled for 2 s against a 200 ms deadline answers `504 action-timeout` in under 1.5 s.

## Gates at the head

`action_resolution` 4/4 · `external_actions` 5/5 · `ingest_diff` 5/5 · `timeout` 4/4 · `registry` 13/13 · `parity_http_mcp` 7/7 · `clippy -p conveyor-actions -p conveyor-api -p conveyor-server --all-targets -- -D warnings` clean · `cargo fmt --all -- --check` clean.

## P4.1 — the one open DoD item, flagged not claimed

The live coding/worker/billing contract receipt is **not** produced here. It needs the deployed upstreams' real contracts and a receipt at the deployed sha; this unit proves the resolution model against the fixture service, and I did not take a live receipt, so none is claimed. It is unrelated to the code this PR changes. Owner decision needed: accept the deferral or run P4.1 as its own live slice.

